<$fill $name="body">

!! Why bundle mind-elixir instead of fetching from a CDN?

Reliability and offline support. TiddlyWiki users frequently run wikis offline or behind firewalls. Bundling the library inside the plugin removes any network dependency and keeps the plugin self-contained for upload/share.

!! Will icons render natively?

mind-elixir doesn't have a first-class icon field. The adapter prepends `core:icon` (an emoji string) to the `topic` text. For colour-block icons or richer styling, future work could install a custom mind-elixir plugin via `me.install()`.

!! Why is `applyOps` false in v1?

mind-elixir's operation API isn't a 1:1 match for our Op vocabulary, and the cost of a full `refresh(data)` is tolerable at v5.11.0. Promoting `applyOps` to `true` requires careful mapping (e.g. `addNode` → `me.addChild(node, parent)`) and per-op error handling. Deferred until the v1 contract proves stable.

!! Can I install a custom mind-elixir plugin (theme, plugin via `me.install()`)?

Not yet through configuration. Until v0.2.x, the adapter does not expose a plugin-install hook. If you need this immediately, fork the adapter and add the `me.install(plugin)` calls in `Engine.prototype.init` after the `this.me = new MindElixirCtor(meOptions)` line.

!! What about cross-links?

The adapter passes `crossLinks` to mind-elixir as `linkData` for round-trip preservation, but mind-elixir's built-in cross-link rendering requires explicit `me.linkDiv()` calls and a specific data shape we don't yet emit fully. Cross-links are preserved but invisible in v0.1.0. Drawing them is on the roadmap.

!! mind-elixir feels editable already — why does `capabilities.edit = false`?

mind-elixir's inline editing emits `operation` events; we capture and route those into our overlay store, so technically gesture-based editing works. The flag is conservative: it tells the host widget not to assume the engine fully implements an "edit" surface API beyond op emission. The flag may flip to `true` once the contract is exercised.

</$fill>
</$transclude>
"},"$:/plugins/rimir/mindmap-elixir/doc/overview":{"title":"$:/plugins/rimir/mindmap-elixir/doc/overview","created":"20260511000000000","modified":"20260511000000000","rdt.hub-category":"visualization","rdt.hub-category-name":"Visualization & Layout","rdt.hub-order":"21","rdt.order":"1","rdt.plugin-key":"mindmap-elixir","rdt.plugin-name":"mindmap elixir","rdt.plugin-subtitle":"mind-elixir engine adapter for rimir/mindmap","rdt.section":"Overview","type":"text/vnd.tiddlywiki","text":"<$transclude $tiddler="$:/plugins/rimir/doc-template/templates/page">
<$fill $name="body">

!! What it does

`rimir/mindmap-elixir` is a `module-type: mindmapengine` adapter that lets `rimir/mindmap` render via [[mind-elixir|https://github.com/SSShooter/mind-elixir-core]] (MIT-licensed, ~85 KB minified). The bundled mind-elixir core is v5.11.0.

!! Key features

* ''Drop-in adapter'' — install alongside `rimir/mindmap`; no configuration required if it's the only engine installed.
* ''Tree-native rendering'' — radial layout, expand/collapse, focus, drag, keyboard shortcuts.
* ''Op translation'' — mind-elixir's `operation` bus events are translated into the canonical Op vocabulary and appended to the view's overlay tiddler.
* ''Round-trip safe'' — unknown-namespace attrs (`mx:*`, `kn:*`, `cy:*`) survive the engine even when not rendered.
* ''Bundled library'' — mind-elixir IIFE + CSS shipped inside the plugin; no CDN or build step required.

!! What is mind-elixir?

A modern, tree-native mindmap library by [[SSShooter|https://github.com/SSShooter]]:

* MIT-licensed
* Active development (v5.11.0 released April 2026)
* Pluggable architecture with `me.install(plugin)`
* Built-in themes, custom CSS variable overrides
* Rich runtime API (`addChild`, `insertSibling`, `removeNode`, `bus.addListener("operation")`)

!! Prerequisites

* `$:/plugins/rimir/mindmap`
* TiddlyWiki 5.3.0+

</$fill>
</$transclude>
"},"$:/plugins/rimir/mindmap-elixir/doc/reference":{"title":"$:/plugins/rimir/mindmap-elixir/doc/reference","created":"20260511000000000","modified":"20260511000000000","rdt.order":"3","rdt.plugin-key":"mindmap-elixir","rdt.plugin-name":"mindmap elixir","rdt.plugin-subtitle":"mind-elixir engine adapter for rimir/mindmap","rdt.section":"Reference","type":"text/vnd.tiddlywiki","text":"<$transclude $tiddler="$:/plugins/rimir/doc-template/templates/page">
<$fill $name="body">

!! Engine identifier

This adapter registers as `MindElixir`. Use this name in:

* `$:/config/rimir/mindmap/default-engine`
* `mm.engine` on view tiddlers
* `engine="MindElixir"` on a `<$mindmap>` widget

!! Engine options (passed via `mm.options`)

|!Key |!Type |!Default |!Effect |
|`direction` |0/1/2 |2 |0=LEFT, 1=RIGHT, 2=SIDE (both sides) |
|`draggable` |boolean |true |Allow node drag-reparenting |
|`contextMenu` |boolean |true |Show mind-elixir's right-click menu |
|`toolBar` |boolean |true |Show mind-elixir's built-in toolbar |
|`keypress` |boolean |true |Bind keyboard shortcuts (Tab=child, Enter=sibling, Delete=remove, F2=rename) |

Additional mind-elixir options pass through verbatim. See [[mind-elixir docs|https://docs.mind-elixir.com]].

Example view tiddler:

```
mm.producer:  knowledge-tree
mm.args:      {"area":"llm"}
mm.engine:    MindElixir
mm.options:   {"direction":2, "draggable":true, "keypress":false}
mm.overlay:   $:/state/rimir/mindmap/views/llm/overlay
```

!! Op translation table

|!mind-elixir operation |!Emitted Op |!Notes |
|`finishEdit` |`{ op: "rename", id, label }` |Emitted when the user finishes inline editing |
|`removeNode` |`{ op: "removeNode", id }` |Explicit user delete |
|`addChild` |`{ op: "addNode", parent, node }` |New node created in canvas |
|`moveNode` |`{ op: "reparent", id, newParent }` |Drag-reparent |
|`moveNodeBefore` |`{ op: "reparent", id, newParent }` |Drag-reorder before sibling |
|`moveNodeAfter` |`{ op: "reparent", id, newParent }` |Drag-reorder after sibling |
|`beginEdit`, `selectNode`, `expandNode` |(none) |Informational, no overlay write |
|other |`{ op: "setAttr", id, key: "mx:lastOp", value: name }` |Round-trip preservation only |

!! Flag-attribute support

The `rimir/mindmap` 0.2.13+ flag decorator stamps engine-specific `mx:*` attrs and the engine-neutral `mx:flags` / `mx:flag-classes` channels. The adapter translates them in two places:

!!! In `toMENode()` → inline `style.*`

|!MDOM attr |!mind-elixir style property |!Notes |
|`mx:text-color` |`style.color` |Overrides the `core:color` auto-contrast colour |
|`mx:font-weight` |`style.fontWeight` |Strings like `bold`, `600` |
|`mx:font-style` |`style.fontStyle` |Typically `italic` |
|`mx:text-transform` |`style.textTransform` |`uppercase` / `lowercase` / `capitalize` |
|`mx:opacity` |`style.opacity` |`"0.5"` etc. |
|`mx:border` |`style.outline` + `outlineOffset: 2px` |//Outline, not border// — the topic bubble's `border` is used for the focus ring |
|`mx:font-size-scale` |`style.fontSize` as `<n>em` |E.g. `mx:font-size-scale: "1.2"` → `style.fontSize = "1.2em"` |
|`mx:icons` |Prepended to `meNode.icons` |Pipe-separated glyphs (decorator-ordered DESCENDING priority); `core:icon` joins on the right |

Empty / unset attrs do not pollute `meNode.style` — every branch guards on truthy value.

!!! In `_applyTooltips()` → DOM `data-*` attrs

|!MDOM attr |!DOM attribute |!Use |
|`mx:flags` |`data-mm-flags` |Space-separated flag names. Match individual flags via `me-tpc[data-mm-flags~="name"]` |
|`mx:flag-classes` |`data-mm-flag-classes` |Custom class tokens from `mm.flag-class` |
|`core:synthetic` (=== `true`) |`data-mm-synthetic="yes"` |Distinguishes synthetic group nodes (chain/axis) from tiddler-backed leaves |

The native `title=` attribute on each `<me-tpc>` is also driven from MDOM attrs: `_applyTooltips` joins the producer's `core:tooltip` and the flag-decorator's `mx:flag-tooltip` with a newline (core first, flag block below). If both are absent the attribute is removed, so empty hovers don't render an empty native bubble.

Each is stamped only when the source attr is non-empty; the adapter clears stale attrs when a previously-flagged node no longer matches. CSS authors get the full token-list semantic without needing to know the engine's internal class structure.

!! Public method surface

|!Method |!Effect |
|`init(element, mdom, options)` |Construct, mount, bind events |
|`update(mdom)` |Replace data via mind-elixir's `refresh()` |
|`focus(id)` |Select the node in mind-elixir |
|`expand(id)` / `collapse(id)` |Toggle a branch |
|`destroy()` |Remove listeners and DOM |
|`on("op", fn)` |Subscribe to translated overlay ops |
|`on("select", fn)` |Subscribe to selection events (fn receives node id) |

</$fill>
</$transclude>
